.TH CHTTPSVR_RESP_ADD_HEADER 3 "2026-09-29" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_resp_add_header \- add one more field of a response header
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_resp_add_header(chttpsvr_resp *resp,
                                        const char *name,
                                        const char *value);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Adds one "name:value" field line to the response, after every field
that is already set.  Unlike chttpsvr_resp_set_header(3), it keeps any
existing fields of the same name, so a response can carry, for
example, two Set-Cookie lines.  The fields go on the wire in the order
of the calls.

It validates name and value exactly as chttpsvr_resp_set_header(3)
does, with the same results: name must be a non-empty RFC 7230 token,
value must contain no CR byte and no LF byte, "Transfer-Encoding" is
refused, and a "Content-Length" value must be a number.

Three names carry at most one field in any response: "Connection",
"Content-Length" and "Date".  For those three, this function behaves
like chttpsvr_resp_set_header(3) and replaces the value; see that page
for how the server treats each of them.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args in the cases that chttpsvr_resp_set_header(3)
lists.
ccol_not_enough_memory when the underlying allocation fails.
A failed call changes nothing in the response.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
chttpsvr_resp_add_header(resp, "Set-Cookie", "a=1; Path=/");
chttpsvr_resp_add_header(resp, "Set-Cookie", "b=2; Path=/");
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_resp_set_header (3),
.BR chttpsvr_resp_write (3)
